-- the table of the Remote database resolves for the user (SHOW COLUMNS is granted)
id	UInt64
-- SELECT through it is rejected without SELECT on the underlying table (prints 1 if rejected)
1
-- INSERT through it is rejected without INSERT on the underlying table (prints 1 if rejected)
1
-- both work once the rights on the underlying table are granted
1
2
-- a table the caller cannot see at all stays hidden: an existing and a missing name are
-- indistinguishable through resolution (prints UNKNOWN_TABLE for both, three times each)
UNKNOWN_TABLE
UNKNOWN_TABLE
UNKNOWN_TABLE
UNKNOWN_TABLE
UNKNOWN_TABLE
UNKNOWN_TABLE
-- SHOW TABLES does not leak the underlying table name without SHOW TABLES on the underlying database (prints nothing)
-- EXISTS TABLE does not leak the existence of the underlying table either (prints 0)
0
-- SHOW TABLES lists the table once SHOW TABLES on the underlying database is granted, even without SHOW COLUMNS
t
-- EXISTS TABLE answers from the name only, so it works without SHOW COLUMNS as well (prints 1)
1
-- ...but DESCRIBE is still rejected without SHOW COLUMNS on the underlying table (prints 1 if rejected)
1
-- a Remote database over another Remote database lists the names without resolving the structure
t
-- ...and it needs no grants on the intermediate database, only on the tables it finally proxies
t
1
-- ...but the tables invisible in the innermost database stay hidden (prints nothing, then 0)
0
-- DESCRIBE through the chain needs no grants on the intermediate database either (prints id UInt64)
id	UInt64
-- reading and writing the data, in contrast, needs the rights on every hop of the chain (prints 1, 1)
1
1
-- ...and then they work through the whole chain (prints 1, 2, 3)
1
2
3
-- system.tables keeps the row of a table whose structure cannot be fetched, with an empty engine
t	
-- a table of a multi-shard proxy resolves with the grants on the table alone (prints 4)
4
-- ...and only the tables the caller may see are listed
t
